  "text_md": "= 0. From which we obtain,\n![](<2503.08105_pg5_images/imageFile12.png>)\nHence either h or g is a constant Ω. Which is the desired result.\n![](<2503.08105_pg5_images/imageFile10.png>)\n∂2f2 ∂zz\n3 Main Results\n![](<2503.08105_pg5_images/imageFile2.png>)\n![](<2503.08105_pg5_images/imageFile23.png>)\n![](<2503.08105_pg5_images/imageFile17.png>)\n![](<2503.08105_pg5_images/imageFile18.png>)\n![](<2503.08105_pg5_images/imageFile19.png>)\n![](<2503.08105_pg5_images/imageFile13.png>)\n![](<2503.08105_pg5_images/imageFile16.png>)\nProof. If either h or g is a constant on Ω, then it easy to see that the square of analytic function or the square of co-analytic function is harmonic on Ω. Assume that the square of a complex-valued harmonic function is harmonic on Ω. Then\n![](<2503.08105_pg5_images/imageFile15.png>)\n![](<2503.08105_pg5_images/imageFile6.png>)\n![](<2503.08105_pg5_images/imageFile8.png>)\n- Theorem 3.1. Suppose f(z) = h(z) + g(z), is harmonic on Ω. Then f2, is harmonic on Ω if and only if either h or g is a constant on Ω.\n![](<2503.08105_pg5_images/imageFile3.png>)\n![](<2503.08105_pg5_images/imageFile9.png>)\n![](<2503.08105_pg5_images/imageFile21.png>)\n![](<2503.08105_pg5_images/imageFile20.png>)",
